ziah was king of Judah for about a year around 842–841 BCE, the son of Jehoram and Athaliah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahichchhatra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahichchhatra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahichchhatra</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahichchhatra was an ancient city in Bareilly district of Uttar Pradesh, India, remembered as the capital of northern Panchala.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahilyabai-holkar"><span class="enc-name">Ahilyabai Holkar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahilyabai Holkar was the Maratha queen who ruled the Holkar domains of Malwa, later Indore State, from 1767 until her death in 1795.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahiram"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahiram.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahiram</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahiram was a king of Byblos, known from his stone sarcophagus found in the city's royal necropolis in 1923.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahlamu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahlamu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahlamu</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ahlamu were nomadic groups of Syria and Mesopotamia named in cuneiform sources from the 14th century BCE, including the Amarna letters and Kassite letters from…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahlat"><span class="enc-name">Ahlat</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahlat is a town on the north-western shore of Lake Van in eastern Turkey, known in medieval sources as Khilat.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahluwalia-misl"><span class="enc-name">Ahluwalia misl</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ahluwalia misl was one of the twelve Sikh confederacies of 18th-century Punjab, led by Jassa Singh Ahluwalia, who also commanded the Dal Khalsa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-araj"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-araj.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad al-Araj</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Araj was the first Saadi sultan of Morocco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad al-Mansur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Mansur was the Saadi sultan of Morocco from 1578 to 1603, enriched by his victory over the Portuguese at Alcácer Quibir.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mukarram"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad al-Mukarram</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Mukarram was the second ruler of the Sulayhid dynasty of Yemen, succeeding his father Ali al-Sulayhi after Ali was killed by the Najahids in the 1060s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-muqtadir"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad al-Muqtadir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Muqtadir (died 1081) was the Hudid king of the Taifa of Zaragoza from 1046 and its most powerful ruler.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mustain-ii"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad al-Mustain II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Mustain II (died 1110) was the Hudid king of the Taifa of Zaragoza from 1085, the last of his line to rule the city.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-alaq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-alaq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Alaq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Alaq, also called Sultan Ahmad Khan, was a Chagatai khan of the eastern part of Moghulistan from 1487, a son of Yunus Khan, ruling from Turpan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-baba-al-timbukti"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Baba al-Timbukti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Baba al-Timbukti was a celebrated scholar and jurist of Timbuktu in the late sixteenth and early seventeenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-bakhsh-khan"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Bakhsh Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Bakhsh Khan was the first Nawab of Loharu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-bin-rashid-al-mualla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-bin-rashid-al-mualla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad bin Rashid Al Mualla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad bin Rashid Al Mualla was ruler of Umm al-Quwain, one of the Trucial States, from 1929 to 1981.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-fadl-al-qumandan"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Fadl al-Qumandan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Fadl al-Qumandan was a prince of the Abdali ruling house of the Sultanate of Lahej in southern Arabia, a poet and musician who also commanded the sultanate's…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-fanakati"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Fanakati</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Fanakati was a Central Asian Muslim from Banakat who served as chief finance minister of the Yuan dynasty under Kublai Khan for some twenty years.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-i-bey"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad I Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad I Bey was the Husainid bey of Tunis from 1837 to 1855.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-abd-al-aziz-ibn-abi-dulaf"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Dulaf</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz was the most notable ruler of the Dulafid dynasty, which governed the region of Jibal in western Iran under the Abbasid caliphs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Fadlan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Fadlan was an Arab member of the embassy sent by the Abbasid caliph al-Muqtadir to the king of the Volga Bulgars in 921 to 922.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-furtu"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Furtu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Furtu was the chief imam and chronicler of Mai Idris Alooma of the Bornu Empire in the late sixteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-hasan-maymandi"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Hasan Maymandi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Hasan Maymandi was a Persian administrator who served as vizier to the Ghaznavid sultan Mahmud of Ghazni from about 1013 to 1024.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi, known as Ahmad Gragn, was the imam and military leader of the Adal Sultanate who led a jihad against the Ethiopian Empire from 1527 to 1543.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-idris"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Idris</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Idris al-Fasi was a Moroccan Sufi teacher whose reformist teaching stressed the direct study of the Quran and hadith.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-said-al-busaidi"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Said al-Busaidi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Said al-Busaidi was the founder of the Al Bu Said dynasty of Oman.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Tulun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Tulun was a Turkish soldier sent to govern Egypt for the Abbasids in 868 who made himself its independent ruler and founded the Tulunid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-yahya-al-yahsubi"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Yahsubi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Yahsubi was the founder of the Taifa of Niebla in south-western al-Andalus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-jalayir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-jalayir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Jalayir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Jalayir was the Jalayirid sultan of Baghdad and Tabriz from 1382 to 1410, a poet, calligrapher and patron of manuscript painting.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-khan-bangash"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-khan-bangash.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Khan Bangash</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Khan Bangash was the Afghan Nawab of Farrukhabad from 1750 to 1771.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-khan-donboli"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Khan Donboli</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Khan Donboli was khan of Khoy from 1763 to 1786 and the most powerful ruler of the Kurdish Donboli family.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-khan-muqaddam"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Khan Muqaddam</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Khan Muqaddam was the best-known khan of Maragheh, a small khanate south-east of Lake Urmia ruled by the Muqaddam family in the 18th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-khani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-khani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Khani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Khani was a Kurdish poet, scholar and mystic from the Hakkari region who settled at Bayazid and wrote in Kurmanji Kurdish.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-muadzam-shah-of-pahang"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Muadzam Shah of Pahang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad al-Muadzam Shah, born Wan Ahmad, was ruler of Pahang on the Malay Peninsula.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-nami"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-nami.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Nami</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Nami was a Syrian politician of Circassian origin, a son-in-law of the Ottoman sultan Abdul Hamid II, who served as head of the French-mandated State of Syria…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-nizam-shah-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-nizam-shah-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Nizam Shah I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Nizam Shah I was the founder of the Nizam Shahi dynasty and the Ahmadnagar Sultanate in the Deccan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-pasha-of-baghdad"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Pasha of Baghdad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Pasha of Baghdad succeeded his father Hasan Pasha as Ottoman governor of Baghdad in 1723 and ruled, with a short interruption, until 1747.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-qavam"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-qavam.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Qavam</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Qavam, also known as Qavam os-Saltaneh, was an Iranian statesman who served five times as prime minister between 1921 and 1952.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Sanjar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Sanjar was a Seljuk sultan who ruled from Khurasan in the first half of the twelfth century, the last ruler to hold authority over much of the Great Seljuk realm.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sayf-al-nasr"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Sayf al-Nasr</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Sayf al-Nasr was the leader of the Awlad Sulayman's Sayf al-Nasr family, which had long resisted Italian rule in Fezzan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Shah Abdali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Shah Abdali, also known as Ahmad Shah Durrani, was an Afghan commander in Nader Shah's army who founded the Durrani Empire after Nader's death in 1747.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-bahadur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-bahadur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Shah Bahadur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Shah Bahadur was Mughal emperor from 1748 to 1754, the son of Muhammad Shah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-i-of-gujarat"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Shah I of Gujarat</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Shah I was sultan of Gujarat from 1411 to 1442, grandson of the dynasty's founder Muzaffar Shah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-i-wali"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-i-wali.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Shah I Wali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Shah I Wali was sultan of the Bahmani Sultanate of the Deccan from 1422 to 1436.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-massoud"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Shah Massoud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Shah Massoud was an Afghan mujahideen commander of the Jamiat-e Islami, known as the Lion of Panjshir for defending the Panjshir valley against Soviet offensives…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-of-divrigi"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Shah of Divriği</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Shah was the Mengujekid ruler of Divriği in eastern Anatolia in the early thirteenth century, under the overlordship of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-qajar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-qajar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Shah Qajar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Shah Qajar was the last shah of the Qajar dynasty of Iran, coming to the throne as a boy in 1909 after his father was deposed in the Constitutional Revolution.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-simba"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Simba</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Simba was a Nabhani prince of Pate who fled the Sultanate of Zanzibar's control and founded the sultanate of Witu on the Kenyan coast in the 1860s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-yar-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-yar-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Yar Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Yar Khan was the last ruling Khan of Kalat, from 1933 to 1955.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-yasawi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-yasawi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmad Yasawi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmad Yasawi was a Turkic Sufi poet and teacher of the 12th century, based at the town of Yasi, now Turkistan, in southern Kazakhstan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmadil-ibn-ibrahim"><span class="enc-name">Ahmadil ibn Ibrahim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmadil ibn Ibrahim was a Kurdish emir of the Rawwadid family who held Maragheh in north-western Iran under the Seljuk sultans.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmadnagar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmadnagar is a city in Maharashtra, western India, founded in 1494 by Ahmad Nizam Shah I as the capital of the Nizam Shahi sultanate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmadou-ahidjo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmadou-ahidjo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmadou Ahidjo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmadou Ahidjo was a Cameroonian politician from the Muslim north who became prime minister of French Cameroon in 1958 and led it to independence in 1960.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmadu-bello"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmadu-bello.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmadu Bello</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmadu Bello, the Sardauna of Sokoto, was a Nigerian politician and leader of the Northern People's Congress.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmadu-tall"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmadu-tall.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmadu Tall</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmadu Tall was the son and successor of El Hadj Umar Tall and ruler of the Toucouleur Empire from Ségou after his father's death in 1864.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-abdallah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmed-abdallah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmed Abdallah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmed Abdallah was a Comorian politician who headed the government of the French Territory of the Comoros and unilaterally declared its independence on 6 July 1975.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-al-fateh"><span class="enc-name">Ahmed al-Fateh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmed al-Fateh, Ahmad ibn Muhammad Al Khalifa, was the first Al Khalifa ruler of Bahrain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-al-sharaa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmed-al-sharaa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmed al-Sharaa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmed al-Sharaa, earlier known as Abu Mohammad al-Julani, is a Syrian militant leader and politician.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-ali-abdullah-saleh"><span class="enc-name">Ahmed Ali Abdullah Saleh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmed Ali Abdullah Saleh is the eldest son of the Yemeni president Ali Abdullah Saleh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-belkadi"><span class="enc-name">Ahmed Belkadi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmed Belkadi was a Kabyle leader from a family of judges who founded the Kingdom of Kuku in the Djurdjura mountains around 1515.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-bey-of-constantine"><span class="enc-name">Ahmed Bey of Constantine</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmed Bey of Constantine was the last bey of the eastern province of Ottoman Algeria, from 1826.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-bukr"><span class="enc-name">Ahmed Bukr</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmed Bukr was a sultan of the Darfur Sultanate who reigned c. 1682–1722.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-hassan-al-bakr"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmed-hassan-al-bakr.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ahmed Hassan al-Bakr</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmed Hassan al-Bakr was an Iraqi army officer and Ba'athist politician who was President of Iraq from 1968 to 1979.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-karamanli"><span class="enc-name">Ahmed Karamanli</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ahmed Karamanli was an Ottoman cavalry officer in Tripoli who seized power in 1711, killed the Janissary officers and founded the hereditary Karamanli dynasty of pashas.</span></a></li></ul>
